WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.220
Luria was inspired to try to address the question of whether mutations actually happened 

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.330
spontaneously,

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.010
or whether they were induced by the environment. 

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:16.930
One question that immediately pops to mind was, what was so special about this guy,

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:21.150
Salvador Luria, that allowed him to solve the problem that nobody else could solve before him.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.640
And it builds on a general theme of, you know, problem solvers,

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:27.160
probably in science but anywhere else,

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:32.140
which is that, he brought a fresh perspective to the problem,

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:35.150
and his fresh perspective was that he was a trained physicist.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:37.860
And so being a physicist,

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:42.260
he thought about biology through quantitative, sort of mathematical ways,

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:47.140
which was different from biologists who were largely involved in drawing descriptive 

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:51.840
diagrams of, you know, what a feather looked like, or recording where 

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.620
species were found and in what environment.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:59.950
That unusual perspective made 

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.950
him approach, methodologically,

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.150
the problem very different from everybody else.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:10.650
Luria chose to approach this problem through bacteria 

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.650
and their sensitivity to viruses that kill them.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.340
And this was,

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:21.850
you know,

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:27.860
really an amazing leap at the time because of the fact that 

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:32.890
bacteria were not perceived to be relevant to the kinds of 

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.220
traits which had driven the question in the first place.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:35.820
So people,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:36.510
as I said,

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.090
saw,

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:38.670
you know,

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:40.800
apes all looked like the same,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.790
but they occasionally would evolve to become a human,

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:48.890
and how could bacteria provide any insight into that question,

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:54.230
was a major leap by him, that there 

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:58.480
was some commonality between what bacteria do with their genetic material and what 

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:00.560
complex organisms do with theirs.

00:02:01.038 --> 00:02:02.668
The bacteria were thought to be,

00:02:03.708 --> 00:02:04.158
you know,

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:09.898
very simple single cell organisms that you could argue didn't even 

00:02:09.898 --> 00:02:10.578
have a trait;

00:02:10.578 --> 00:02:12.518
all they were was they generated more bacteria.

00:02:13.148 --> 00:02:15.068
So where is the trait involved there?

00:02:14.798 --> 00:02:15.798
But

00:02:16.508 --> 00:02:20.898
people knew that not all bacteria were the same, that they did acquire things, which seem to be 

00:02:21.198 --> 00:02:24.158
genetically inheritable, that distinguished them.

00:02:25.398 --> 00:02:30.388
And one trait that bacteria could acquire that made them different from any other, was 

00:02:30.388 --> 00:02:35.388
their ability to be resistant to a virus that would kill 

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:37.678
them. Like the viruses that infect us,

00:02:37.688 --> 00:02:40.168
there are many different types of viruses that infect bacteria.

00:02:40.708 --> 00:02:44.318
And there was a particular virus that was useful to bacteria,

00:02:44.618 --> 00:02:45.558
which was called T1.

00:02:47.308 --> 00:02:50.558
It was a virus that could,

00:02:51.008 --> 00:02:52.418
after it bound to the surface,

00:02:53.508 --> 00:02:57.918
proliferate within a cell within twenty minutes, and cause the cell to lyse and die.

00:02:58.368 --> 00:03:03.118
It was a killing machine. And, however, 

00:03:03.448 --> 00:03:08.438
it wasn't quite true that all bacteria were killed, there were a few that survived this 

00:03:09.108 --> 00:03:11.828
onslaught of exposure to T1.

00:03:13.038 --> 00:03:17.698
And it turns out that if you took those few bacteria that 

00:03:17.698 --> 00:03:22.538
survived, and you allowed them to propagate and 

00:03:22.538 --> 00:03:26.728
produce lots of babies, lots of progenies, all the babies,

00:03:26.728 --> 00:03:31.488
the progeny, also were no longer sensitive to the presence of the T1 in the 

00:03:31.488 --> 00:03:31.978
solution.

00:03:32.408 --> 00:03:37.398
So these rare bacteria that arose that were unable to be killed by 

00:03:37.398 --> 00:03:42.178
the virus were able to provide-- this trait could then be 

00:03:42.178 --> 00:03:44.018
inherited to all their progeny afterwards.

